  { q: 'Why does Odin worry about his ravens?', a: 'In Grímnismál (stanza 20), Odin says he worries about Huginn that he might not return, but fears more for Muninn. This admission of vulnerability — a god who controls so much is anxious about his birds — is theologically significant. If Huginn (Thought) fails to return, Odin loses his external agency; if Muninn (Memory/Mind) fails, Odin loses something more fundamental — perhaps his identity, perhaps the accumulated knowledge that makes him who he is. The anxiety about Muninn specifically suggests the deeper of the two ravens embodies something essential and potentially fragile about Odin\'s power.' },
